 <!-- Generated by `pnpm generate:plugin-schemas` from src/shared/plugins. Do not edit. -->
 
-Host API 1.3 (`"pluginApi": 1`). Workers call a method with `await orca.host.call(name, params)`; panels and custom editors call the same methods through `window.orca.host.call(name, params)`. Each method lists the surfaces allowed to call it; any other surface gets `not_permitted`. `orca.host.has(name)` answers synchronously whether this Orca offers a method on the current surface, and `orca.api` is `{ major, minor }`. See [Plugin authoring](./plugin-authoring.md) for the frame API.
+Host API 1.4 (`"pluginApi": 1`). Workers call a method with `await orca.host.call(name, params)`; panels and custom editors call the same methods through `window.orca.host.call(name, params)`. Each method lists the surfaces allowed to call it; any other surface gets `not_permitted`. `orca.host.has(name)` answers synchronously whether this Orca offers a method on the current surface, and `orca.api` is `{ major, minor }`. See [Plugin authoring](./plugin-authoring.md) for the frame API.
 
 Every call is checked against the capabilities the user approved, and params are validated before Orca runs anything: params are strict, so a field this Orca does not know is refused with `invalid_params` instead of being dropped. Mutations are recorded in the audit log as `plugin:<publisher>.<id>`.
 
@@ -28,7 +28,7 @@ Methods whose capability is `none` need no capability but still need the plugin
 | [`shell.openExternal`](#shellopenexternal) | none | panel, editor | no | 1.1 | Opens an http:, https: or mailto: URL in the default browser. Needs a click or key press in the frame; desktop app only. Orca asks the person before an http(s) host the plugin was not allowed; `opened` is false if they cancel. |
 | [`files.saveAs`](#filessaveas) | none | panel, editor | yes | 1.1 | Offers data (a string or ArrayBuffer, up to 64 MiB) in Orca's save dialog; the plugin never learns the path. Needs a click or key press in the frame; desktop app only. |
 | [`plugin.request`](#pluginrequest) | none | panel, editor | no | 1.2 | Asks the plugin's own worker to run the handler it registered with `orca.panels.handle(name)`, starting the worker if needed; resolves with what the handler returned. `unknown_method` when no handler has that name. |
-| [`agents.list`](#agentslist) | `agent:read` | worker, panel, editor | no | 1.2 | The agents in the focused workspace: their terminal, title, agent kind and status. `agent.status.changed` reports each change after. |
+| [`agents.list`](#agentslist) | `agent:read` | worker, panel, editor | no | 1.2 | The agents in the focused workspace: their terminal, title, agent kind and status, and `prompt: "question"` while one waits on a question (host API 1.4). `agent.status.changed` reports each change after. |
 | [`events.subscribe`](#eventssubscribe) | none | panel, editor | no | 1.2 | Delivers a workspace or agent event to this panel as `orca.on(event)`; needs the event's read capability. The frame APIs call it for you. |
 | [`statusBar.update`](#statusbarupdate) | none | worker | no | 1.2 | Changes one of the plugin's `contributes.statusBarItems` (named by `id`): fields given replace their current value, `visible: false` hides it. Desktop app only; lasts until Orca restarts, and is hidden while the plugin is disabled. A plugin with `main` and status bar items has its worker started when it is enabled and kept running while it stays enabled, so a worker can poll and update its items without an event or a hook. |
 | [`workspace.list`](#workspacelist) | `workspace:read` | worker | no | 1.3 | Lists every workspace, Git worktrees and folders, on every host Orca reaches. |
